 {:value "15046"} "Middleweight (≤80 kilograms), Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "15118"} "Open Class, Men"]] [:script {} "$('#same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "15351"} "1972"] "\n" [:option {:value "15646"} "1976"] "\n" [:option {:value "16004"} "1980"] "\n" [:option {:value "16385"} "1984"] "\n" [:option {:value "16794"} "1988"] "\n" [:option {:value "17253"} "1992"] "\n" [:option {:value "18008"} "1996"] "\n" [:option {:value "18747"} "2000"] "\n" [:option {:value "19475"} "2004"] "\n" [:option {:value "260537"} "2008"] "\n" [:option {:value "316678"} "2012"] "\n" [:option {:value "354880"} "2016"] "\n" [:option {:value "18001172"} "2020"] "\n" [:option {:value "2007052"} "2024"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/348"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Heavyweight (&gt;80 kilograms), Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "22 October 1964 — 13:00"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/465"} "Nippon Budokan, Chiyoda, Tokyo"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/15091"} "14 from 12 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Round-robin pools advanced judoka to single-elimination tournament."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/33858"} "Isao Inokuma"] " was the favorite, a 5th Dan who was All-Japan Champion. His challengers were felt to be the Canadian " [:a {:href "/athletes/34292"} "Doug Rogers"] ", who had trained with Inokuma in Japan, and the Soviet Georgian, " [:a {:href "/athletes/33912"} "Anzor K’ik’nadze"] ", who had almost defeated Inokuma in 1961, using sambo armlock techniques. There were five pools. Inokuma, Rogers, and K’ik’nadze all won their pools comfortably, as did " [:a {:href "/athletes/33916"} "Kim Jong-Dai"] " of Korea. Inokuma and Kim faced off in the only knock-out (quarter-final) match. Inokuma put Kim to the mat and held him down until Kim tapped submission, which was almost unheard of in that era. Rogers faced the second Soviet, " [:a {:href "/athletes/34448"} "Parnaoz Chik’viladze"] ", in the first semi-final, with Rogers winning with " [:em {} "yusei-gachi"] ". In the second semi-final, Inokuma remembered his near defeat to K’ik’nadze and took no chances of allowing him to get his armlock hold. He threw K’ik’nadze with " [:em {} "tai-otoshi"] " at the five-minute mark and won the match to advance to the final against Rogers. The final was a dull affair, with little wrestling occurring for the first 10 minutes. The referee, Charles Palmer (GBR), warned both judoka, and threatened to disqualify both with neither getting a medal. The pace quickened a little, but not much. Inokuma was given the decision for greater aggressiveness although the result was formally termed " [:em {} "kinsa"] "."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/33858"} "Isao Inokuma"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/JPN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/JPN.png"}] "JPN"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/34292"} "Doug Rogers"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CAN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CAN.png"}] "CAN"]] [:td {} [:sp